.TH PACREACT 1 "2026-11-10" "pacreact 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pacreact \- event-driven HUDs and notification daemon for Wayland
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pacreact
.br
.B pacreact
.I command
[\fIargument\fR ...]
.br
.B pacreact
.RB [ \-h " | " \-\-help " | " \-V " | " \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pacreact
sleeps until something deserves attention, then shows a small floating HUD on a Wayland compositor
that implements wlr-layer-shell.
It reacts to volume and brightness changes, low battery, memory pressure, network state changes,
timers and alarms, and desktop notifications, and it replaces the notification daemon (mako, dunst, ...).
.PP
Run without arguments it is the daemon.
With a command it is a client of the running daemon, which it reaches through the socket
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pacreact.sock .
HUDs are closed and acted on with these commands, typically from compositor keybinds.
A left click on a notification card also dismisses it (the app is sent its
.B default
action first, if it offered one), and a right click dismisses it without invoking anything;
everything else on the HUD passes clicks through.
A card from an app that set the
.B resident
hint is not closed by invoking an action, whether with
.B pacreact action
or by a left click on its default action: it stays until it expires, is dismissed with a right click or
.BR "pacreact dismiss" ,
or the app closes or replaces it.
.PP
HUDs stack in one column in a corner of the screen (top right by default), newest on top, each leaving on its own timeout.
The few things that must not be ignored are kept apart, in the attention deck at the top centre:
notifications of critical urgency, notifications with the category
.BR call.incoming ,
and pacreact's own critical alerts (battery, memory, disk space, temperature).
It holds three, as a pile: the oldest unhandled one in front in full and never reordered by itself, the others as one-line strips behind it.
When the front one leaves, the next comes forward; clicking a strip brings that card forward, and right-clicking dismisses it.
A fourth waits for room.
.B action ,
.B expand
and
.B dismiss
work on the front card of the deck before anything in the stack.
The deck needs a screen at least 1204 logical pixels wide when the stack is in a top corner, so that they do not overlap; on a narrower screen, and with
.BR anchor = top ,
attention items go in the stack instead.
At most four are shown.
Alerts (warning and critical toasts, timers, battery and memory alerts, every notification card)
are never lost: when the column is full they wait for room.
Sliders and informational toasts are ephemeral and are dropped, or displace the oldest ephemeral one, when it is full.
Volume and brightness changes appear in a separate on-screen display in the middle of the screen.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR toast " [" \-l " " info | warn | crit "] [" \-t " \fISECONDS\fR] \fITEXT\fR..."
Show a message.
Every HUD in the stack has the same width, so long text wraps onto up to three lines.
With
.BI \-i " ICON"
it has a status icon: the name of an icon of the theme, or several separated by commas, the first that exists being used.
The same option of
.B slider
gives the bar one.
.B warn
and
.B crit
are alerts and wait for room when the stack is full.
.TP
.BI slider " PERCENT LABEL" ...
Show a level bar.
.TP
.BI timer " DURATION " [ LABEL "...]"
Start a timer.
.I DURATION
is made of numbers with a unit, such as
.BR 90s ", " 5m ", " 1h30m
(units
.BR s ", " m ", " h ", " d ;
at most 30 days).
A unit is required.
Prints the new id.
.TP
.BR alarm " [" \-r " \fIRECUR\fR] [\fIYYYY-MM-DD\fR] \fIHH:MM\fR [\fILABEL\fR...]"
Set an alarm for the next such wall-clock time, today or tomorrow, or, with a date, for that moment.
.I RECUR
is
.BR daily ", " weekdays ", " weekends
or a list such as
.BR mon,wed,fri .
A recurring alarm stays scheduled after it fires.
A dated alarm cannot repeat.
.TP
.BR peek " [" \-r " \fIRECUR\fR] \fIHH:MM\fR [\fILABEL\fR...]"
Show the time quietly at that time of day, like glancing at a clock: a small toast, no sound, not in the history, and not at all while do-not-disturb is on.
With
.B \-r
it repeats like an alarm.
A peek that is late is skipped.
.TP
.BR peek " every \fIDURATION\fR [\fILABEL\fR...]"
The same after that much time at the computer, again and again (at least 1m).
Time at the computer stops counting after
.B peek_idle
minutes without input, and starts over after a break of
.B peek_break
minutes.
This needs the compositor's idle notifications; without them it counts the time since the daemon started.
.TP
.B print
List the print jobs that have not finished: id, state, queue and name.
Asks CUPS when run.
.TP
.B time
Show the time and the date in the middle of the screen for a moment.
.TP
.BR snooze " [\fIDURATION\fR]"
Re-arm the timer or alarm that last went off as a new one-shot (default 5m, same label).
.TP
.BI pause " ID"
Freeze a timer's remaining time.
.TQ
.BI resume " ID"
Continue a paused timer.
.TQ
.BI extend " ID DURATION"
Add time to a timer or alarm (to the time left, if paused).
.TP
.B list
Show timers and alarms with their ids, time left and recurrence.
.TP
.BR cancel " \fIID\fR | " all
Cancel one or all.
.TP
.B dismiss
Close the front card of the attention deck, else the newest alert on screen, or the newest item if there is none.
.TP
.B expand
Open the front card of the attention deck, else the newest notification card, to show more of its body, or close it again (it prints
.B expanded
or
.BR collapsed ).
An open card does not expire until it is closed.
Resting the pointer on a card does the same for as long as it stays there (after a quarter of a second), and holds the card's timeout meanwhile; that needs
.B click
not to be 0.
Toasts and sliders do not open.
.TP
.B count
Print how many notification cards are on screen or waiting for room.
Toasts and sliders are not counted.
Meant for status bars, in the way of
.B makoctl list
and
.BR "dunstctl count displayed" .
.TP
.BR history " [\fIN\fR | " clear ]
List the last notifications, newest first: when, what happened to it (on screen, expired, dismissed,
closed by the app, or skipped by do-not-disturb), and the app, summary and body.
Shows 10 by default and at most 64, as many as fit in one reply.
.B clear
overwrites them with zeros.
Notifications marked transient are not kept.
The history is a fixed ring of 64 entries held in memory only: the oldest is overwritten when it is full and
nothing is written to disk.
.TP
.BR dnd " [" on " [\fIDURATION\fR] | " off " | " toggle " | " status ]
Do not disturb.
While on, notification cards below critical urgency are not shown and make no sound; they are counted
and the total is reported when it is switched off.
Timers, alarms, battery and memory alerts and sliders are not affected, except that the opt-in sounds for power, devices and network are held back.
The state is kept in memory only.
.TP
.BR action " [\fIN\fR]"
Invoke action
.I N
(default 1) of the notification card on screen: the application is told, and the card is closed.
Cards list their actions as numbered hints, for example
.BR "[1] Reply  [2] Archive" .
.PP
Commands exit with status 0 on success, 1 if the daemon refused the request (the reason is printed to
standard error) or cannot be reached, and 2 for a usage error.
.SH SETTINGS
Set in \fIpacreact.conf\fR (see FILES), as \fIkey = value\fR lines.
.TP
.B click
Set to
.B 0
to make the whole HUD click-through, cards included.
.TP
.B anchor
Where the HUD stack sits:
.BR top\-right " (the default), " top\-left ", " top ", " bottom\-right ", " bottom\-left " or " bottom .
.TP
.B output
Show HUDs on this monitor (for example
.BR DP-1 ).
Unset lets the compositor choose.
An unknown name is reported once, with the names that exist.
.TP
.B osd
Set to
.B stack
to show the volume and brightness bars and the clock of
.B pacreact time
in the stack with the notifications, not in the middle of the screen.
.TP
.B contrast
Set to
.B high
for a black background, white text and brighter colours, with nothing dimmed. The layout is the same.
.TP
.B icons
Set to
.B 0
to turn the status icons off: the icon theme's icons that go with pacreact's own messages (battery, network, headset, volume and the like).
SVG ones are drawn by running rsvg-convert, when it is installed. They are found as SVG or PNG files in the GTK theme, hicolor, Adwaita and AdwaitaLegacy.
Set to
.B theme
to take them only from the theme: by default the volume, microphone, webcam, printer, headphones and headset ones are Nerd Font glyphs when the font is installed.
.TP
.B icon_font
The font file of those glyphs (Nerd Fonts version 3). Unset looks for SymbolsNerdFont-Regular.ttf in the font directories. Without the font the theme's icons are used.
.TP
.B icon_size
The size of the status icons in pixels, 12 to 128 (default 24 at the default font size, scaled with it).
.TP
.B margin
The distance from the screen's edge to the stack, and to the top of the attention deck, in pixels, 0 to 200 (default 16), or a length with a unit: mm, in, pt, px or % of the screen's shorter side (\fImargin = 5mm\fR). font_size, width and icon_size take lengths too.
.TP
.B font_size
The size of the text in pixels, 10 to 48 (default 16).
Everything scales with it: the fonts, padding, icons, bars and the width of the cards, toasts and sliders, so 20 makes the HUD 1.25 times as large.
.TP
.B text_size_min
.TP
.B text_size_max
The smallest and the largest the smallest text on the HUD may be, as a length with a unit (\fItext_size_min = 3mm\fR); the whole HUD grows or shrinks with it, whatever font_size says. Not wider than the screen. Unset: no limit.
.TP
.B speech
yes (default) or no: speak what the HUD shows through speech-dispatcher. Only in a build with SPEECH=speechd.
.TP
.B width
The width of the cards, toasts and sliders in the stack in pixels, 200 to 1200, on its own (default: follows the font size, 380 at 16).
.TP
.BR font ", " font_bold
Font files for Latin text instead of the default; without a bold file, bold is made from the regular one.
Other scripts keep their own fonts.
A path that is not a readable regular file is reported and ignored.
.TP
.B scale
Pin the render scale, 1 to 4, fractions allowed.
By default the compositor's preferred scale is followed, per monitor.
.TP
.B fade_ms
Fade duration in milliseconds (default 120).
.B 0
disables the fade.
.TP
.B probe_host
An IPv4 address to ping for the network slowdown alert.
Without it no probe is made.
.TP
.B probe_interval
Seconds between probes, 5 to 3600 (default 30).
.TP
.B startup_grace
Seconds after start-up during which a memory or battery alert that is not worse than one shown in the
last 30 minutes is not shown again (default 60;
.B 0
disables it).
.TP
.B clock_every
Minutes between the times the clock (as in
.BR "pacreact time" )
shows itself in the middle of the screen, counted from the hour: 30 is at :00 and :30 (default 30, 1 to 60;
.B 0
turns it off). Not shown while do-not-disturb is on.
.TP
.B peek_idle
Minutes without keyboard or pointer input after which you count as away from the computer for
.BR "peek every" ,
1 to 120 (default 5).
.TP
.B peek_break
Minutes away that start the count of use over, 1 to 720 (default 15).
.TP
.B print
.B 0
turns the printing toasts (a job printing, finished or failed, a printer out of paper, jammed, low on ink, offline) off;
.B done
leaves out the one for a job starting to print.
.TP
.B print_debug
.B 1
prints what CUPS and the system bus said, on standard error.
.TP
.B usb
.B 0
turns the toasts for USB devices (webcams, printers, drawing tablets, drives, headsets, keyboards and mice) off.
A comma separated list of kinds
.RB ( camera ", " printer ", " tablet ", " storage ", " audio ", " input ", " other )
announces only those.
.TP
.B jacks
.B 0
turns the audio-jack toasts (headphones, headset, microphone, line out) off.
.B generic
makes every headphone or microphone jack plug say "Audio device connected" or "Audio device disconnected",
for hardware that reports one connector as two jacks.
.TP
.B jacks_debug
.B 1
prints each jack switch event, card port change, volume reading and decision, with timestamps, on standard error.
.TP
.B sound
.B 0
turns all sound off.
.TP
.B player
Program started with a sound file as its only argument (default
.BR pw-play ,
else
.BR paplay ).
.TP
.B sound_volume
Sound for a change of the output volume or mute (the moments the volume bar appears; not the microphone).
Off by default.
.B 1
(or
.BR on )
plays the theme's
.BR audio-volume-change ;
anything else is a sound name or an absolute path.
Do-not-disturb does not hold it back.
.TP
.BR sound_power ", " sound_devices ", " sound_network
Sounds for the charger being plugged or unplugged, for a headset, headphones, microphone, USB device or Bluetooth device
connecting or disconnecting, and for the network coming online or going offline.
All are off by default, and only changes make a sound, not the state at start-up.
.B 1
(or
.BR on )
plays the sound theme's own pair for the event
.RB ( power-plug / power-unplug ,
.BR device-added / device-removed ,
.BR network-connectivity-established / network-connectivity-lost ).
A name or absolute path plays for both directions;
.I UP,DOWN
gives each its own, and an empty half is silent.
Empty or
.B 0
means silent.
They are held back while do-not-disturb is on.
.TP
.BR alarm_sound ", " notify_sound ", " alert_sound
Sound for timers and alarms (default
.BR alarm-clock-elapsed ),
for notification cards, and for battery and memory alerts (both off by default):
an absolute file path or a name from the freedesktop sound theme.
Empty or
.B 0
means silent.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LANGUAGE ", " LANG ", " LC_MESSAGES
Select the language of the HUD messages.
.TP
.B PULSE_SERVER
A
.B unix:
socket of the sound server to use instead of the default.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Where timers are saved.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I pacreact.conf
Optional settings, read from /etc/pac/pacreact.conf, ~/.config/pac/pacreact.conf (or $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/pac/) and ./pacreact.conf, each overriding the earlier. The file in the source folder lists the defaults. Settings are one \fIkey = value\fR per line (\fIfont_size = 22\fR). They are not read from the environment. Read at start-up.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pacreact.sock
The daemon's command socket.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/pacreact/alerts
The last memory and battery alert shown, for the start-up grace period.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/pacreact/timers
Timers and alarms, restored when the daemon starts (default
.IR ~/.local/state/pacreact/timers ).
Ones that came due while the daemon was not running fire once.
.TP
.I ~/.config/gtk-3.0/settings.ini
The icon theme to look notification icons up in, tried before hicolor and Adwaita.
.SH NOTIFICATIONS
pacreact implements the
.B org.freedesktop.Notifications
service, so only one notification daemon can run at a time.
It supports summary and body (tags removed), urgency, replacement by id, sound hints, actions,
and icons as raw pixels, as PNG paths or as icon-theme names (PNG only).
.PP
A left click on a card's default action also sends the app an
.B ActivationToken
signal, just before
.BR ActionInvoked ,
with a token from the compositor (the
.B xdg-activation-v1
protocol) so that the app can take focus.
Without that protocol, or for actions invoked with
.BR "pacreact action" ,
only
.B ActionInvoked
is sent.
.PP
With no timeout requested (\-1) cards stay up 3 s (low urgency) or 6 s (normal); critical cards never expire.
A timeout of 0 means never expire, for any urgency; any other value is honoured as asked, but never below 1.5 s.
A card that never expires stays until it is clicked, dismissed with
.BR "pacreact dismiss" ,
closed by the app or replaced.
.PP
.B CloseNotification
for an id that is not on screen returns a D-Bus error.
A notification suppressed by do-not-disturb is closed at once with reason 4, so apps that wait for it do not hang.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
pacreact timer 5m tea
pacreact alarm \-r weekdays 07:30 gym
pacreact alarm 2026\-12\-25 07:30 present
pacreact snooze 10m
pacreact dnd on 1h
pacreact action 2
.fi
.PP
In Hyprland, for instance:
.nf
hl.bind("SUPER + Escape", hl.dsp.exec_cmd("pacreact dismiss"))
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pw\-play (1),
.BR paplay (1),
.BR notify\-send (1)
.SH BUGS
Battery, charger and backlight events have only been exercised on a desktop machine without a battery
or backlight.
TCP sound servers are not supported.
